A||||10||BBBAD GT||Suspension opinions wanted||||1||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||02-02-2000||02:08 PM||||<BR>I've been hearing/reading some conflicting opinions on what to do to the suspension.  Some people say to first upgrade the cradle bushing to steel, and others say to replace all rubber bushing with poly ones.  I've also heard a rear sway bar is needed after the bushing go on.  Any opinions on what to do first without going hog wild (yet) with springs/struts are appreciated.  I'm going to retire the Mustang from autocross duty (its not that fun or fast steering with your right foot), and hopefully get my new 87 GT to make me proud.<P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Eric||02-02-2000||04:48 PM||||I think the first thing to do would be to get some good shocks on there.  A lot of people use konis for autocross.  You definatly want to get a rear sway bar.  Poly bushings are good, but they can make your daily commute a little rough.  I don't have any suspension mods yet, and my car does just fine around the cones.  Be careful what you do.  Someone may find out what your car is riding on and take you out of Cstock.||||reg||1
Z||000002||lowCG||02-02-2000||07:39 PM||||All of the really fast autocross cars are pretty tail-happy,so the rear swaybar is a good idea for that,the rear control arm bushings are the most easily squished rubber on the car,will crisp up the shifts,make the car more predictable at the limit,I wouldn't go out of my way to replace the cradle bushings.And the shocks are probably the most important,after the tires.I've never experienced the Koni's with the stock springs,but it sounds like there's enough adjustment in those shocks to make it all work.If you do the rear swaybar,make sure to not over tighten the outer bushings/links to the point where you bind up the movement of the suspension,that'll make it handle like a truck.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||02-02-2000||08:13 PM||||Metal cradle bushings are for racing only. If this will be street driven at all, forget about that. First, make sure to replace any worn parts like ball joints and tie rod ends. I like the Moog brand, they seem to hold up well. Next, do what has already been said, rear sway bar, good tires, and good shocks. Poly bushings are nice, but if your class rules don't allow it and if your old bushings are shot, just replace them with stock rubber ones. They'll hold up for a few years. Maybe not quite as firm as poly, but better than worn out rubber ones that are falling apart.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||02-03-2000||12:45 AM||||Why would you say that the metal cradle bushings are for racing only?  The factory '84-'87 cradle bushings are rubber and cause things to "move" when driving, especially hard driving.  In '88 they remedied this by mounting the cradle solidly to the frame....no more bushings.  This firmed up the suspension quite a bit, but not to the point of overharshness.  When you put solid bushings on the early cradles (be it poly or aluminum or ??) you are merely creating a solid mount to the frame, a la '88 Fiero.<P>I agree that the effort required to install these bushings is great and I wouldn't do it unless you had the engine/cradle out for some other reason, but it certainly isn't for racing only.  It will help make the earlier Fieros handle better and in a more predictible manner   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||02-03-2000||11:02 AM||||Well... I'm glad somebody is out there to keep me honest. I didn't know that the 88 models had solid cradle mounts, but I suspect that there are other changes to the suspension to make up for this fact.<P>I probably should not of declared that it was ONLY for racing. I should of said, mostly for racing or high performance driving (which SCCA events would qualify). I personally have not used the solid cradle mounts, but I've heard that under frequent extreme driving conditions that the aluminum bushings bolt holes can became elonged and sloppy over time.<P>There was a guy on the Forum that posted a topic recently who had stiffened up his suspension so much that the rear control arms were flexing. Something has to give and it's supposed to be in the bushings not bending metal parts. If I were to do this mod, I'd go with the poly bushing as a good compromise.||||reg||1
Z||000006||jlhuber||02-03-2000||05:54 PM||||what about these polygraphite bushings that i keep hearing of?  has anyone tried them?  if so, they any good?  the ad says they act like plain poly bushings but with the added benefit of graphite to constantly lubricate them.  <A HREF="http://www.p-s-t.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.p-s-t.com</A>  is a site that offers them.  if they are decent, i might have to finally raplace the original rubber bushings on my 86 gt with polygraphite.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||02-03-2000||11:24 PM||||Those poly-graphite bushings are just regular poly bushings impregnated with graphite in order to keep them from squeaking. I used them on my sway bar. This is a good thing because poly bushings have a nasty habit of making a lot of noise as they age.<P>I just looked at that link you provided, I didn't see any Fiero stuff at that site. I bought my poly control arm bushings from the below listed site. They were helpful, inexpensive, and fast. Unfortunately, these poly bushings are not the graphite impregnated type, but they do come with a special grease that is supposed to help mute the squeaks.<P> <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm</A> <p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||02-04-2000||01:16 PM||||Ed Parks told me that the 88 went to solid cradle mounts because they realised that there were two levels of vibration/shock damping on the earlier models.  The cradle bushings and the engine/trans mounts both serve the same function as far as vibration/shock damping is concerned.||||reg||1
Z||000009||BBBAD GT||02-04-2000||01:22 PM||||So, anybody here actually have the solid bushings on the cradle?  Just curious....||||reg||1
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||02-04-2000||01:29 PM||||I have urethane bushing on the torque strut, all suspension points, front sway bar (after hearing the controversy over the rear sway I think I will try it and see if it makes a difference.) and the cradle. For a daily driver I have never had a complaint. Before I replace the bushing the car had been pretty beat up and the bushing in the back were pretty sloppy. I have been very happy with them daily for 3 years. Yes they did up the vibration for me a bit but not uncomfortably. Though I do have to go back and lube up the rear A-arms for squeaking.<BR>I chose urethane over aluminum or steel bushing because of cost. I have driven a lowered 88 GT with all aluminum (Chris Sideris) and didn't feel much if any difference in handling or vibration.||||reg||14
